# BMAD customization overrides are deliberate configuration, not installed
# module content: `_bmad/custom/.gitignore` ignores only `*.user.toml`, so the
# non-user overrides are meant to be shared. The `_bmad/*` exclusions above are
# written with a trailing `*` rather than as a bare directory so git descends
# far enough for these negations to apply.
!_bmad/custom/
_bmad/custom/*
!_bmad/custom/*.toml
# ...but never personal overrides. `*.user.toml` ends in `.toml`, so the
# negation above would otherwise re-include it. Do not rely on the inner
# `_bmad/custom/.gitignore` for this -- it is not itself tracked.
_bmad/custom/*.user.toml
.bmad-loop/policy.toml

# Grok CLI (bmad-loop review adapter). Untracked, like .claude/ and _bmad/.
# Grok's own README claims repo-scoped skills are filtered when gitignored;
# grok 1.0.30 does not do that -- the skill is discovered under `.grok/skills/`,
# a bare `skills/` rule and `.grok/` alike. Worktree checkouts are served by
# `seed_files = [".grok/skills"]` in .bmad-loop/profiles/grok.toml, which copies
# the tree in, so nothing here needs to be tracked.
.grok/*
# ...except the bmad-loop hook registration. `install.py` writes this file only
# at `bmad-loop init`, and worktree reuse means an existing run never re-seeds
# it, so the SessionEnd removal that keeps grok dev sessions alive survives in
# exactly one place on disk and nowhere in git. Tracking the one file makes it
# recoverable from a clone; the 944-file `.grok/skills/` tree stays ignored,
# per this repo's rule against re-adding vendored tool docs.
!.grok/hooks/
.grok/hooks/*
!.grok/hooks/bmad-loop.json
